/* Debug view styling — task 20-rules-core/06.
   Palette taken from build/playtest/index.html so the Unity build and the
   HTML prototype look the same; the same codes are listed in art-prompts.md
   section 1. Layout lives here, not inline: knowledge/unity/03-ui-toolkit-runtime.md
   recommends class-based styling. The one exception is per-tile background
   colour, which DebugGameView computes from the kind id at runtime. */

.game__root {
    flex-grow: 1;
    background-color: #F4EAD8;
    /* padding-top was 16 and padding-bottom 16 until 2026-08-28. SafeArea
       already holds the whole panel clear of the notch and the home
       indicator, so this was a second inset on top of the first, paid out of
       the pile's height on the one screen where height is scarce (rooms
       09-12 carry 60-tile piles; see the header note below). */
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 4px;
    padding-left: 12px;
    padding-right: 12px;
    align-items: center;
}

/* =====================================================================
   60-shell-build/01: the header, rebuilt 2026-08-28.

   What it was: two centred lines of prose — "Room 1 of 12 · pile 1 of 1"
   at 20px bold across two thirds of the width, and "Items left: 36" under
   it — with the kitten exiled to a 56-unit corner beside them. The owner's
   words: the sentence eats the top of the screen and the cat is a tiny
   icon you cannot make out.

   What it is now: ONE row, as tall as the kitten, holding a cream plaque
   of numerals on the left and the kitten at 104 units on the right. No
   sentence, no words at all — see NOTES.md for the games this was read
   off and for what each fact turned into.

   The height arithmetic, because this screen has no slack (panel is
   390x844 units, PanelSettings match=1 so height is fixed):
     usable height after the safe area (Dynamic Island, 59 + 34)  751
     - header                                                    104
     - shelf (5 + 32 + 5 padding, 8 margin)                        50
     - root padding-bottom                                          4
     = for the pile                                               593
   A 60-tile pile is 6 columns x 10 rows; at the tile pitch below
   (52 + 3 + 3) that is 580. Thirteen units to spare. The old layout, at
   the old pitch of 60, needed 600 and had 592 — which is why the bottom
   tile row is the first thing to check in a room-09 screenshot.
   ===================================================================== */
.game__header {
    width: 100%;
    flex-direction: row;
    /* Top-aligned, not centred: the plaque lines up with the back button
       (top: 4) while the kitten stands the full height of the row. */
    align-items: flex-start;
    height: 104px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Reserved for HouseMapView's back-to-map plaque, which is absolutely
   positioned at left:4/top:4 and 44 wide, and so takes no room in this
   flow. 52 leaves 4 units of air between it and the plaque beside it.
   If that button is ever moved or resized, this number moves with it. */
.game__header-gap {
    width: 52px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.game__header-spacer {
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/* The one plaque, over a photograph. Opaque cream on ink, the same pair
   the back button and the map's room plaques already use: cream #F6EEDC
   on ink #332A1E is 12.2:1 by the WCAG relative-luminance formula, and
   because the plaque is opaque that ratio holds over any room photo. A
   translucent scrim would have made it depend on the picture behind it. */
.game__hud {
    margin-top: 4px;
    padding-top: 4px;
    padding-bottom: 5px;
    padding-left: 9px;
    padding-right: 9px;
    background-color: #F6EEDC;
    border-left-width: 2px;
    border-right-width: 2px;
    border-top-width: 2px;
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-left-color: #332A1E;
    border-right-color: #332A1E;
    border-top-color: #332A1E;
    border-bottom-color: #332A1E;
    border-top-left-radius: 12px;
    border-top-right-radius: 12px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 12px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 12px;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

.game__hud-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

/* "5/12" — the room, as a fraction rather than a clause. The denominator
   survives where the shipped games drop it (their level counts are
   unbounded; twelve rooms is a promise the player can hold). */
.game__room-count {
    font-size: 17px;
    color: #332A1E;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

/* One pip per pile in THIS room, filled up to and including the one being
   played. This is "pile 2 of 3" with the words taken out, and it is the
   same count the room's cleaned quadrants behind the pile already show. */
.game__pips {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-left: 8px;
}

.game__pip {
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    margin-left: 2px;
    margin-right: 2px;
    background-color: #F4EAD8;
    border-left-width: 1px;
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-left-color: #7C6A52;
    border-right-color: #7C6A52;
    border-top-color: #7C6A52;
    border-bottom-color: #7C6A52;
    border-top-left-radius: 4px;
    border-top-right-radius: 4px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 4px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 4px;
}

.game__pip--done {
    background-color: #4A3B28;
}

/* The pile, as a bar that EMPTIES, next to the count that falls with it.
   Both move the same way on purpose: a bar that fills while the number it
   sits beside drops would be two readings of one fact in opposite
   directions. */
.game__bar {
    width: 74px;
    height: 7px;
    margin-top: 4px;
    background-color: #D9C6A3;
    border-top-left-radius: 4px;
    border-top-right-radius: 4px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 4px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Width is set from C# as a percentage; everything else lives here. */
.game__bar-fill {
    height: 100%;
    background-color: #4A3B28;
    border-top-left-radius: 4px;
    border-top-right-radius: 4px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 4px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 4px;
}

.game__left-count {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: #4A3B28;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    margin-top: 4px;
    margin-left: 7px;
}

/* The pile: tiles wrap into rows, newest additions flow left to right. */
.game__pile {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: flex-start;
    flex-grow: 1;
    max-width: 360px;
}

/* The shelf sits at the bottom, one row, never wraps.
   Sized to fit: nine slots at 44px plus their margins came to about 502
   units against a 390-unit panel (Shell/PanelSettings.asset), so the two end
   slots were cut off the screen — visible in every iOS screenshot until
   2026-08-27. The slot is 32 wide now, and shrinks further rather than
   overflowing on a narrower phone. */
.game__shelf {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    background-color: #E8D9BD;
    border-top-left-radius: 10px;
    border-top-right-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 10px;
    /* padding 6 and margin-top 12 until 2026-08-28: six units of the
       height the 60-tile rooms do not have, spent on air the shelf's own
       border already provides. */
    padding-top: 5px;
    padding-bottom: 5px;
    padding-left: 6px;
    padding-right: 6px;
    margin-top: 8px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.game__slot {
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
    /* Unity defaults flex-shrink to 0, unlike the web: without this the row
       overflows the screen instead of giving way. */
    flex-shrink: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    margin-left: 2px;
    margin-right: 2px;
    background-color: #F4EAD8;
    border-left-width: 2px;
    border-right-width: 2px;
    border-top-width: 2px;
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-left-color: #C9A97C;
    border-right-color: #C9A97C;
    border-top-color: #C9A97C;
    border-bottom-color: #C9A97C;
    border-top-left-radius: 8px;
    border-top-right-radius: 8px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 8px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 8px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

.game__tile {
    width: 52px;
    height: 52px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    margin-right: 4px;
    /* Vertical margins 4 -> 3 on 2026-08-28, and ONLY the vertical ones.
       A 60-tile pile (rooms 09-12) is ten rows; two units a row is twenty
       units of height back, which is most of what the taller header costs.
       Left/right stay at 4 so the column pitch is still 60 against the
       360-unit max-width — six columns, unchanged, no reflow. The tile's
       own 52 is untouched, so DebugGameView's TileHalf (26) and .game__fly
       (52) still describe it. */
    margin-top: 3px;
    margin-bottom: 3px;
    background-color: #C9A97C;
    border-top-left-radius: 10px;
    border-top-right-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 10px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* D3: a buried item hides its kind. Blank tile, not a faded one —
   the player must not be able to read what is underneath. */
.game__tile--hidden {
    background-color: #B0A79B;
}

/* Reachable but not takeable: the PROP dims, the tile does not.
   opacity on the tile dimmed its children too, so the lock badge faded along
   with the item it was there to mark. Tinting the background image leaves
   child elements at full strength. */
.game__tile--dim {
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
}

/* =====================================================================
   60-shell-build/01: motion. Every animation below is a USS transition
   over an inline style or a class the C# toggles — not experimental.animation
   (see NOTES.md 2026-08-28 for why).

   The hard constraint this had to be designed around: Render() calls
   _pileArea.Clear() and _shelfArea.Clear() on every move, so the tile that
   was tapped and the slot it lands in are both DESTROYED before any
   animation on them could run. So the travelling tile and the match pop are
   not the real elements — they are throwaway copies on .game__fx-layer,
   which Render() never touches. The refusal flinch is the one exception:
   a refused tap does not redraw, so the real tile survives and animates.
   ===================================================================== */

/* Sits over the whole board, never intercepts a tap, never redrawn. */
.game__fx-layer {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
}

/* The travelling copy of a tapped tile: pile position -> shelf slot.
   left/top rather than translate: transitions on both are supported, and
   StyleLength takes a plain float from C# with no struct constructor whose
   signature could differ between Unity versions. One element per tap, so
   the layout cost of animating position is not worth a risk to compiling. */
.game__fly {
    position: absolute;
    width: 52px;
    height: 52px;
    border-top-left-radius: 10px;
    border-top-right-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 10px;
    transition-property: left, top, width, height;
    transition-duration: 150ms;
    transition-timing-function: ease-out;
}

/* Arriving: the copy shrinks from tile size (52) to slot size (32) as it
   flies, so it visibly becomes a shelf item rather than landing oversized. */
.game__fly--landed {
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
}

/* A match: the three slots that emptied pop and vanish. Deliberately a
   different verb from a placement — that one travels, this one expands and
   fades on the spot, so the two are never confused at a glance. */
.game__pop {
    position: absolute;
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
    border-top-left-radius: 8px;
    border-top-right-radius: 8px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 8px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 8px;
    scale: 1;
    opacity: 1;
    transition-property: scale, opacity;
    transition-duration: 170ms;
    transition-timing-function: ease-out;
}

.game__pop--out {
    scale: 1.7;
    opacity: 0;
}

/* A flash of the shelf's own border colour behind the popping prop, so a
   match reads even when the props are pale against the shelf. */
.game__pop-flash {
    background-color: #C9A97C;
}

/* A refused tap — locked, buried, or the board is over. The tile flinches
   and comes back: 90ms away, 110ms back, and the player has learnt that the
   game saw the tap and said no. Without this the tile is silent and
   "refused" and "frozen" look identical from the outside. */
.game__tile--refused {
    scale: 0.86;
    rotate: -5deg;
}

/* Both directions of the flinch are transitions of the same property set;
   the class toggle supplies the two ends. */
.game__tile {
    transition-property: scale, rotate;
    transition-duration: 110ms;
    transition-timing-function: ease-out;
}

.game__tile-label {
    font-size: 18px;
    color: #4A3B28;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

/* Overlay is present in the tree from the start and hidden by default,
   so showing it is a class toggle rather than a rebuild. */
.game__overlay {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    background-color: rgba(74, 59, 40, 0.55);
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

.game__overlay--shown {
    display: flex;
}

.game__card {
    background-color: #F4EAD8;
    border-top-left-radius: 14px;
    border-top-right-radius: 14px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 14px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 14px;
    padding-top: 20px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    padding-left: 24px;
    padding-right: 24px;
    align-items: center;
    min-width: 260px;
    /* Until 2026-08-29 there was no maximum, and nothing noticed while the game
       spoke two languages. Measured across seventeen on a 1080px screen: the
       card ran 830px in English and 1062px in Indonesian — nine pixels of margin
       a side. Nothing was clipped; the card had simply stopped being a card and
       become a panel.

       The widener was the title, which had no width of its own to obey and so
       laid itself out on one line however long that line came out. The body was
       already capped at 240.

       320 leaves the body its designed width plus the 48 of padding, and holds
       35 units of margin a side on a 390-unit panel. */
    max-width: 320px;
}

.game__card-title {
    font-size: 22px;
    color: #4A3B28;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    /* Wraps, now that there is an edge to wrap against. A German or Indonesian
       title on two centred lines is a title; the same title pushing the card to
       the screen edge is a layout bug. */
    white-space: normal;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.game__card-body {
    font-size: 15px;
    color: #6B6055;
    white-space: normal;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
    max-width: 240px;
}

.game__card-button {
    font-size: 16px;
    color: #4A3B28;
    background-color: #C9A97C;
    border-top-left-radius: 10px;
    border-top-right-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 10px;
    padding-top: 10px;
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    padding-left: 20px;
    padding-right: 20px;
    margin-top: 6px;
    border-left-width: 0;
    border-right-width: 0;
    border-top-width: 0;
    border-bottom-width: 0;
    min-width: 200px;
}

/* The lock badge sits in the lower-right corner of its tile, over the prop
   and clear of most of it. Not stretched across the tile: prop_locked is a
   coil of rope drawn on its own, so at full size it replaces the prop
   instead of marking it, and the player loses the one thing the lock is
   supposed to tell them — which kind is withheld (D15, task 40-art/02). */
.game__tile-lock {
    position: absolute;
    right: -2px;
    bottom: -2px;
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
}

/* 60-shell-build/04: the cat portrait. 60-shell-build/01, 2026-08-28: she
   is no longer a 56-unit badge pinned to the corner but the right-hand end
   of the header row, at 104 — the largest single thing on the board that is
   not a prop.

   TWO elements, not one, and that is not decoration. DebugGameView.Paint
   sets background-color: clear on whatever it paints, so any background
   declared for .game__cat was being wiped the moment her art loaded — the
   cream disc this rule used to carry has never once appeared on a screen.
   The seat is a separate parent that Paint never touches, so its disc
   survives; .game__cat is the child that holds the art.

   THE SEAT IS ALSO THE TAP TARGET. 60-shell-build/15 (share card) is going
   to make her open a full-screen card: that handler goes on the seat, which
   is 104x104 — well past the 48x48dp Material asks for and the 44x44pt iOS
   asks for. The disc is what makes it look pressable before anything is
   wired to it. Nothing is registered on it yet; this task only made the
   room. */
.game__cat-seat {
    width: 104px;
    height: 104px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    /* A soft cream disc, so a grey tabby is never lost against a grey room
       photograph, and so the shape reads as one object worth pressing. */
    background-color: rgba(246, 238, 220, 0.82);
    border-top-left-radius: 52px;
    border-top-right-radius: 52px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 52px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 52px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

.game__cat {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
}

/* 60-shell-build/06: the win card's before/after. Two panes, side by side,
   between the card title and its body. */
.game__before-after {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
}

.game__ba-pane {
    align-items: center;
    margin-left: 8px;
    margin-right: 8px;
}

.game__ba-collage {
    width: 116px;
    height: 116px;
    border-top-left-radius: 10px;
    border-top-right-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 10px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 10px;
}

/* "Before" reads as clutter: a muddier tone behind an overlapping scatter of
   props. "After" reads as order: a lighter tone behind a tidy row — the same
   colour language the pile (dirty) and the shelf (clean) already use
   elsewhere in this file. */
.game__ba-pane--before .game__ba-collage {
    background-color: #B0A79B;
}

.game__ba-pane--after .game__ba-collage {
    background-color: #F4EAD8;
    border-left-width: 2px;
    border-right-width: 2px;
    border-top-width: 2px;
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-left-color: #C9A97C;
    border-right-color: #C9A97C;
    border-top-color: #C9A97C;
    border-bottom-color: #C9A97C;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-content: flex-start;
    padding-top: 4px;
    padding-left: 4px;
}

.game__ba-item {
    width: 30px;
    height: 30px;
}

.game__ba-item--messy {
    position: absolute;
}

.game__ba-item--tidy {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    margin-right: 2px;
    margin-bottom: 2px;
}

.game__ba-label {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: #7C6A52;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    margin-top: 4px;
}

/* =====================================================================
   60-shell-build/28: the first lesson.

   The owner watched living people play and none of them worked out that
   the game is about three of a kind. So the first pile now walks one
   real move, and everything below is how that move is pointed at.

   No holes are cut in the dark, because UI Toolkit has no masking and
   because geometry chasing three tiles would have to be recomputed on
   every layout pass. Instead: one sheet over the room photograph, a dim
   class on every tile except the three, and a ring on the one being
   asked for. Nothing here is measured or positioned from C#.

   These rules come LAST in the file on purpose. `.game__tile--dim`
   (reachable but not takeable) can be on the same tile at the same
   specificity, and the later rule is the one that wins — during the
   lesson the lesson decides how a tile looks.
   ===================================================================== */

/* Over the room photograph, under everything the player reads. A child
   of the room layer, so it inherits the negative safe-area insets that
   already carry that layer out to the glass. */
.game__lesson-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    background-color: rgba(20, 15, 9, 0.62);
}

/* Not part of the lesson: still on the board, still legible as an
   object, but plainly not what is being asked for. Opacity rather than
   the background tint `.game__tile--dim` uses — that one has to leave a
   lock badge at full strength, and this one has nothing to protect. */
.game__tile--lesson-dimmed {
    opacity: 0.26;
}

/* The same, for the two controls that are not tiles: the kitten and the
   way back to the map. They live in the header, which is in front of the
   room and so in front of the scrim, and on the first screenshot run they
   sat at full brightness while answering nothing at all — a bright button
   that ignores a finger is a worse thing to put in front of a new player
   than a dim one. */
.game__lesson-dimmed {
    opacity: 0.26;
}

/* Tap THIS one. A heavy ink ring in the cream of the shelf, on a tile
   that is at full brightness while its neighbours are at a quarter. */
.game__tile--lesson-cue {
    border-left-width: 3px;
    border-right-width: 3px;
    border-top-width: 3px;
    border-bottom-width: 3px;
    border-left-color: #F6EEDC;
    border-right-color: #F6EEDC;
    border-top-color: #F6EEDC;
    border-bottom-color: #F6EEDC;
    scale: 1.06;
}

/* The breathing half of the ring. USS has no keyframes, so FirstLesson
   toggles this class on a timer and `.game__tile`'s own scale transition
   (110ms, further up this file) does the moving. */
.game__tile--lesson-cue-on {
    scale: 1.18;
}

/* Where it went. Shown only during the stop after the first take.

   Scale and colour, deliberately NOT a border: `.game__shelf` declares no
   border width, so a cue would have to add one, and in UI Toolkit a
   border eats the content box — nine 32-unit slots in a row that just
   lost six units of width is a reflow of the one element the player is
   being told to look at. Scale moves the ink and leaves the layout
   alone, the same reason `.game__tile--refused` uses it. */
.game__shelf--lesson-cue {
    background-color: #F6EEDC;
    scale: 1.05;
}

/* The phrase. Low on the screen because the three props are high — ids
   1, 5 and 8 land in the top two rows of a six-wide pile, and a caption
   must never cover the thing it points at. */
.game__lesson-word {
    position: absolute;
    left: 12px;
    right: 12px;
    bottom: 70px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

.game__lesson-word-text {
    font-size: 20px;
    color: #F6EEDC;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    white-space: normal;
    padding-top: 8px;
    padding-bottom: 8px;
    padding-left: 14px;
    padding-right: 14px;
    background-color: rgba(20, 15, 9, 0.72);
    border-top-left-radius: 12px;
    border-top-right-radius: 12px;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 12px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 12px;
}
